## 1 Step-by-step build recap

### 1.1  Environment choices

| Component        | Final version       | Why                                                                                                 |
| ---------------- | ------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Python**       | 3.10 via deadsnakes | Torch 2.5 wheels compiled for 3.10/3.11, not 3.12.                                                  |
| **PyTorch**      | 2.5.0 + cu121       | First CUDA-12.x build Torch-AO 0.11 expects.                                                        |
| **TorchAO**      | 0.11.0              | Exposes `quantize_` + `int4_weight_only`. ([github.com][5])                                         |
| **Transformers** | `main` (≥ 4.53-dev) | Adds Gemma-3 architecture & SigLIP wrapper. ([huggingface.co][1])                                   |
| **Accelerate**   | 0.27.x              | Needed when `device_map` is used.                                                                   |
| **Safetensors**  | bypassed            | Incompatible with Torch-AO tensor subclass; saved via `safe_serialization=False`. ([github.com][6]) |

### 1.2  Problems & fixes

| Issue                       | Error symptom               | Resolution                                              |
| --------------------------- | --------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Old Transformers wheel**  | `KeyError: 'gemma3'`        | Upgrade to nightly. ([huggingface.co][1])               |
| **TorchAO import errors**   | `cannot import quantize_`   | Pin TorchAO 0.11 + Torch 2.5.                           |
| **CUDA OOM during quant.**  | 11 GB used, +240 MB scratch | Shift quantisation to **CPU**, keep BF16.               |
| **Safetensors writes fail** | `invalid python storage`    | Save with `safe_serialization=False`. ([github.com][6]) |
| **INT4+TP mis-align msgs**  | “input size not aligned …”  | Use TP = 1 or 2 (divides 128). ([github.com][7])        |

